This looks like a CBP ruling on the "Mamba" power cable (Shunyata Research). It is an electrical power cord fitted with connectors, for a voltage of 110-120V, so it falls under 8544.42 (other electric conductors, fitted with connectors, for a voltage not exceeding 1,000 V).

The ruling I recall classified it under 8544.42.9090, which is "Other electric conductors, for a voltage not exceeding 1,000 V, fitted with connectors: Other: Other". However, there is a split between 8544.42.20 (coaxial cable) and 8544.42.90 (other), and I believe a power cord with connectors falls under 8544.42.90. The statistical suffix for 8544.42.9090 is "Other."

{"hts_code": "8544.42.9090", "reason": "A power cord made of 3-conductor cable and fitted with connectors, for a voltage not exceeding 1,000 V, is classified in heading 8544 as an insulated electric conductor fitted with connectors (subheading 8544.42, other)."}
